UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Week 4, Lesson 3: Spelling and editing strategies', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Spelling and editing strategies include sounding syllables, checking homophones (their/there/they’re), testing word families and running a slow proofread pass for one error type at a time. Keep a personal error pattern list.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nCorrect and name the strategy: ''The visitors left there bags by the door while Mara stoped smiling.''\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: their; stopped; strategies: homophone check + doubling rule.\n\nCHECK\nOnly running spellcheck and accepting every suggestion. Writers still verify meaning.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nProofread a page with three labelled passes and a personal error-pattern note of two recurring mistakes.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Spelling and editing strategies include sounding syllables, checking homophones (their/there/they’re), testing word families and running a slow proofread pass for one error type at a time. Keep a personal error pattern list.","lesson_number":3,"lesson_role":"Language and vocabulary","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN4-ECB-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Proofread a page with three labelled passes and a personal error-pattern note of two recurring mistakes. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using spelling strategy on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Only running spellcheck and accepting every suggestion.. Correction: Strong answer: their; stopped; strategies: homophone check + doubling rule.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Only running spellcheck and accepting every suggestion. Writers still verify meaning. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating spelling strategy on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with spelling strategy, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about spelling strategy with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for spelling strategy. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for spelling strategy. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Only running spellcheck and accepting every suggestion","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","their; stopped; strategies: homophone check + doubling rule"],"question":"Correct and name the strategy: ''The visitors left there bags by the door while Mara stoped smiling.'' Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for spelling strategy; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["their; stopped; strategies: homophone check + doubling rule","Careful use of homophone and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Only running spellcheck and accepting every suggestion","Spelling and editing strategies include sounding syllables, checking homophones (their/there/they’re), testing word"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with spelling strategy?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named homophone and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Only running spellcheck and accepting every suggestion","Name homophone and explain why it matters only, without linking it to spelling strategy","Apply spelling strategy to new material and name the homophone and explain why it matters used"],"question":"Which response best applies spelling strategy with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries spelling strategy. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Say the writing is good without naming any words","Only running spellcheck and accepting every suggestion","Point to exact words in the sentence that show spelling strategy and say what they do","Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event"],"question":"Read: \"Under the pier, Sam found a locked tin and a dripping map.\" Which choice best uses spelling strategy from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill with textual evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name homophone and explain why it matters without linking it to any words in the text","their; stopped; strategies: homophone check + doubling rule","Only running spellcheck and accepting every suggestion","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged"],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about spelling strategy?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Locate precise textual evidence, then explain how language choices shape meaning or effect","Guess from the title alone with no textual clue","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged","Lengthen the answer with adjectives while keeping the same idea"],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to analyse homophone and explain why it matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess","Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length","Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea","Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake"],"question":"Which revision actually improves meaning for spelling strategy?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Accurate transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about spelling strategy","Retype the worked model as the final answer","Change fonts and claim the work is new","Only running spellcheck and accepting every suggestion"],"question":"Which response shows accurate use of spelling strategy on fresh material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about spelling strategy with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating spelling strategy on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using spelling strategy on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for spelling strategy. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with spelling strategy, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Only running spellcheck and accepting every suggestion.. Correction: Strong answer: their; stopped; strategies: homophone check + doubling rule.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s4-eng-y7-w04-l3","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise spelling strategy.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use spelling strategy with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify homophone and explain why it matters.","I can practise proofread pass independently on a new prompt.","I can notice error pattern needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Spelling and editing strategies include sounding syllables, checking homophones (their/there/they’re), testing word families and running a slow proofread pass for one error type at a time.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Spelling and editing strategies include sounding syllables, checking homophones (their/there/they’re), testing word","A guess with no explanation","A claim unrelated to the text"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about spelling strategy with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nPass 1: end punctuation. Pass 2: homophones. Pass 3: doubled letters in past tense (stopped, planned). Example fix: ''There display'' → ''Their display''.\n\nTRY FIRST\nCorrect and name the strategy: ''The visitors left there bags by the door while Mara stoped smiling.''\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: their; stopped; strategies: homophone check + doubling rule.","explain":"Spelling and editing strategies include sounding syllables, checking homophones (their/there/they’re), testing word families and running a slow proofread pass for one error type at a time. Keep a personal error pattern list.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Only running spellcheck and accepting every suggestion. Writers still verify meaning. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Proofread passes.","caption":"Slow and focused.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Pass</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">one error type</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Homophone</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">their / there</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Pass 1: end punctuation. Pass 2: homophones. Pass 3: doubled letters in past tense (stopped, planned). Example fix: ''The Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Pass 1: end punctuation. Pass 2: homophones. Pass 3: doubled letters in past tense (stopped, planned). Example fix: ''The Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.","options":["Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does","A claim unrelated to the text","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no explanation"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about spelling strategy with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nPass 1: end punctuation. Pass 2: homophones. Pass 3: doubled letters in past tense (stopped, planned). Example fix: ''There display'' → ''Their display''. Think about how this word or relationship creates meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nUnderline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","explain":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Saying a choice is good without naming the actual detail does not show understanding. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Explain the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Error patterns.","caption":"Do not trust tools blindly.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Pattern</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">personal list</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Verify</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">meaning still right</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison","A guess with no explanation","A claim unrelated to the text"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about spelling strategy with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nOnly running spellcheck and accepting every suggestion. Writers still verify meaning. Here is a more productive model: Pass 1: end punctuation. Pass 2: homophones. Pass 3: doubled letters in past tense (stopped, planned). Example fix: ''There display'' → ''Their display''.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach would weaken your work, then write a corrected version.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: their; stopped; strategies: homophone check + doubling rule.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"A superficial change in length or appearance does not repair meaning. Only running spellcheck and accepting every suggestion. Writers still verify meaning. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example","A guess with no explanation","A claim unrelated to the text"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about spelling strategy with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nSpelling and editing strategies include sounding syllables, checking homophones (their/there/they’re), testing word families and running a slow proofread pass for one error type at a time. Keep a personal error pattern list. Now choose different people, facts or text to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake one fresh example demonstrating the skill and explain your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should meet the same purpose as the model, but contain original material.","explain":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new content. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Transfer to an independent example"}],"title":"Year 7, Week 4, Lesson 3: Spelling and editing strategies","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Narrative: short stories and voice","week":4,"week_in_unit":4,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nPass 1: end punctuation. Pass 2: homophones. Pass 3: doubled letters in past tense (stopped, planned). Example fix: ''There display'' → ''Their display''.\n\nExplain what makes this example accurate and effective, identifying its specific wording.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Proofread passes.","caption":"Slow and f